  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:xT_VJyRLcyI",
    "purpose": "Step-by-step demonstration of cooking arroz con pollo (chicken and rice) in a rice cooker, with side explanations on onion cutting, jarred olives, whole versus ground spices and saffron.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Arroz con pollo can be made almost hands-off in a rice cooker by sweating aromatics, coating the rice in oil and spices, adding broth at the right ratio, and cooking the vegetables and chicken on top.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Aromatics (onion, garlic) are sweated in olive oil on the rice cooker's quick cook setting. If your cooker won't heat with the lid open, do this step in a skillet.",
      "Uses a cup of long-grain rice to about 1⅓ cups of chicken broth, with the rice coated in oil and spices before cooking.",
      "Vegetables (carrots, green beans, peas) and boneless skinless chicken thighs sit on top of the rice. Frozen pre-cut vegetables work just as well.",
      "Saffron, cumin and paprika season the dish. Saffron is optional.",
      "Cooks about 35 minutes on white rice mode. The chicken is then shredded with two forks and folded back in with sliced olives and cilantro.",
      "Asides explain why the horizontal onion cut makes more even pieces and that most pimientos in stuffed olives are gelled pepper juice.",
      "Also explains why whole spices keep their aroma longer than pre-ground ones.",
      "The dish is flexible: other vegetables, corn, capers or raisins can go in. What matters is the rice-to-liquid ratio and oiling the rice."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction and preparation",
        "summary": "Introduces arroz con pollo and the rice cooker approach, starts olive oil heating, and dices onion (with a defense of the horizontal cut) and garlic.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Preparing vegetable ingredients",
        "summary": "Prepares carrots, green beans, peas and pimiento-stuffed olives, noting frozen vegetables are fine and explaining jellied pimientos. Then measures cumin and paprika and mentions the Our Place rice cooker collaboration.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Combining ingredients",
        "summary": "Seasons the sweated aromatics with salt, MSG and spices, discusses whole versus pre-ground spices, then adds long-grain rice, chicken broth, vegetables and chicken thighs.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "Adding saffron and cooking",
        "summary": "Realizes the saffron was forgotten, removes the chicken, adds crushed saffron while explaining its cost and role, then starts the white rice cycle.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 14
      },
      {
        "title": "Shredding and final assembly",
        "summary": "After 35 minutes, shreds the chicken with two forks, removes the rice, and folds in olives and cilantro. Suggests variations, then adjusts seasoning with salt, MSG and olive oil.",
        "first_segment": 15,
        "last_segment": 18
      },
      {
        "title": "Outro and resources",
        "summary": "Sign-off, then a pitch for kenjilopezalt.com, Patreon, books, products and live events.",
        "first_segment": 19,
        "last_segment": 19
      }
    ],
